  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air analysis late Monday \nevening reveal a fairly chaotic synoptic pattern atop the CONUS, \nwith an open shortwave trough beginning to eject onto the High \nPlains and shortwave ridging upstream over the west coast states. At \nthe surface, broad high pressure encompasses essentially all areas \neast of the Rocky Mountains, resulting in light southeast winds \nacross our neck of the woods. Latest KDDC radar scans show light \nreturns have developed along and west of US-83 ahead of the \nshortwave impulse, but given dry air near the surface, \nprecipitation is likely not reaching the ground. As \ntemperatures continue to drop overnight and sublimating \nprecipitation adds moisture to the lower levels, the first light\nsnowfall of the season may materialize along and west of US-83 \nin the next few hours through mid-morning Tuesday. That said, \nthe open and progressive nature of the wave will significantly \nlimit any snowfall as NBM probability of greater than 0.5\" is \n25% or less for all areas. Otherwise, modest height falls, \novercast skies for much of the day, and light southerly winds \nwill foster below normal temperatures with afternoon highs in \nthe upper 30s/low 40s. Overnight, clearing skies and light winds\nwill optimize radiational cooling allowing temperatures to \nplummet down to the low 20s by Wednesday morning.\n\nDaytime Wednesday, upper level shortwave ridging will stall out over \nthe Rocky Mountains as a much stronger vorticity max dives south-\nsoutheast through the High Plains and cuts off near the Ozarks. \nHowever, the ridge will be close enough for subsidence ahead of it \nto foster 850-mb temperatures rising by 2-4 degrees C, which will \ntranslate to afternoon highs increasing into the mid 40s east to the \nlow 50s west under clear skies.\n\nThursday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles \nsuggest weak longwave ridging will continue west of the Rockies \nwhile a longwave trough deepens over the central CONUS by the \nweekend. This synoptic evolution will favor a rapid warming trend \nacross southwest KS as afternoon highs spike from the mid/upper 40s \non Thursday to the upper 50s/low 60s on Friday and Saturday before \ncooling off a bit into the 50s on Sunday and Monday. Precipitation \nchances will also be scarce as ensemble meteograms indicate no \nreliable QPF signal through early next week.",
